From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 03:05:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: compile the device library offline into a
 metallib

Creating a device built every program of the device library from its MSL.
Metal's shader cache holds the results only after a process has compiled
them once, so every cold start, and every start after the cache was
evicted, paid a front-end compile of the whole library before the device
existed: on an M-series Mac a cold vkCreateDevice took 1.85 s.

kk_clc now also writes each program's MSL into one bundle, and Apple
builds compile that bundle with the target platform's Metal toolchain into
a metallib that the driver carries as data. Each kernel is named after its
program index. Creating a device loads the library from that data and
builds each pipeline from its compiled function, and still compiles a
program's MSL if the build made no metallib or Metal refuses one of its
functions. Other targets build no metallib.

The 25 programs make a 209 KiB metallib. A cold vkCreateDevice now takes
0.64 s, which is Metal's back-end compile to GPU code that only its shader
cache can skip, and a warm one 3 ms instead of 5 ms. The process's Metal
cache keeps 260 KiB instead of 792 KiB, since it no longer stores the
compiled libraries. A device test checks that every program comes from the
metallib.
